<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Debal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Daybul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Debal was a port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, close to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, often identified with the excavated site of Banbhore. Active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a>, it was the first city taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> in his conquest of Sindh in 711–712, after attacks on Arab shipping nearby had given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> a pretext for the invasion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/debal.jpg" alt="Debal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shahid1024 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_Jinnah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Debal, also written Daybul, Daibul or Dēwal, was a commercial town and port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. Arabic histories of the early eighth century record it as Daybul. One view derives the name from the Sanskrit Devalaya, abode of God; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chach-nama">Chach Nama</a> derives it from Dēwal, temple, after a temple that stood there. Its people were mostly of the Med tribe, and at the time of Muqaddasī's visit its merchants spoke Sindhi and Arabic.</p><p>Its exact site has long been uncertain. The maps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> and Istakhri place it on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> at the shore. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>, around 1600, held that Debal was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a>, as did most early British writers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a>; H. M. Elliot in 1867 preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> and placed the temple on Manora Island, while Malcolm Robert Haig in 1894 argued for the ruins of Kakar Bukera on the Baghar branch of the Indus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to modern archaeologists Debal was founded in the first century CE and soon became the most important trading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, home to thousands of Sindhi sailors, among them the Bawarij. The tenth-century geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> described its huts, the dry land around it that supported little farming, and how well its people kept their fishing vessels and trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> built its first large stone structures, a city wall and a citadel, and an earthquake in 893 is reported to have destroyed the port.</p><p>Debal was the first city stormed by Muhammad bin Qasim. The ninth-century historian Al-Baladhuri recorded a massacre of part of the population that lasted three days, after which Qasim marked out a quarter of the town for a Muslim garrison. In 1221 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din Mangburni</a> entered it unopposed when its ruler Hasrar fled. Soon afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> changed course and the town declined; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>'s description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in the early 1300s does not mention it, and its trade passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahari-bandar">Lahari Bandar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavations at Banbhore between September 1958 and April 1965, directed by F. A. Khan, found 14 Kufic inscriptions on the collapsed northern wall of its congregational mosque. A study of them by Muhammad Abdul Ghafur in 1966 concluded strongly that Banbhore was Debal, as N. B. Baluch had argued in 1952 on philological grounds without archaeological evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Debal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sindh's ports, such as <b>Debal</b>, were active in Indian Ocean trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/brahman-dynasty-of-sindh">Brahman dynasty of Sindh</a></li></ul></section>
